E-Commerce Order & Inventory Manager

Java Assignment — Storing, Finding, Ordering and Managing Data

1. Assignment Goal
Build a console-based E-Commerce Order & Inventory Management System.
The system stores products, customer orders, product categories, orders waiting to
be shipped, orders that were already delivered, and customer reviews. Each of these
kinds of data behaves differently and has different requirements: some must be fast
to look up, some must never contain duplicates, some must remember the order in

which things happened, and some must be processed in a strict first-come-first-
served sequence.

Your main job is to decide how each kind of data should be stored and how
objects should be ordered. The assignment describes the behavior the system
must have. It is up to you to choose the Java types and techniques that deliver that
behavior, and to be able to defend every choice you make.

2. Learning Objectives
By the end of this assignment you should be able to:
• Analyse a business requirement and decide how the data behind it should be
stored.
• Provide fast retrieval of a single record when the program only knows its ID.
• Guarantee that a group of values contains no duplicates.
• Process waiting work in strict first-in, first-out order.
• Preserve the exact sequence in which events happened, when that sequence
matters.
• Give a class of objects a default ordering, and also support a completely
different ordering for another class without touching that class’s own code.
• Safely remove elements from a group of stored objects while you are looping
over it.
• Represent a value that can only ever be one of a small, fixed set of named
states.
• Store and manage your own custom objects rather than plain text or
numbers.
• Justify every storage and ordering decision you made.

3. Required Classes
Product
Attributes: int id, String name, double price, String category, int
stockQuantity
Required behavior: appropriate constructors, getters and setters, and a
toString() that prints the product in a readable form.
Ordering requirement: A product has one default, built-in way of being ordered:
by price, from cheapest to most expensive. Any part of the program that asks for
products “in order” — without saying which order it wants — must get them sorted
by price ascending. This ordering rule belongs to the Product class itself; the code
that performs the sorting must not have to explain how two products compare.
CartItem
Attributes: Product product, int quantity
Required methods: getProduct(), getQuantity(), calculateSubtotal(). The
subtotal is the product price multiplied by the quantity.
Order
Attributes: int orderId, String customerName, the items belonging to this order,
double total, and the current status of the order.
Required methods: addItem(), removeItem(), calculateTotal(),
displayOrder(), and a method to update the order status.
Storage of the items: The items of an order must be kept in the exact sequence in
which the customer added them, and the same product may need to be reached by
position while you loop through the order.
Automatic total calculation: The order’s total must not depend on the user
remembering to ask for it. Every time addItem() or removeItem() is called, the
method must internally call calculateTotal() before returning, so the total field
is always up to date. displayOrder() simply shows the already-updated total — it
must never be responsible for calculating it.
Order Status
Every order carries a status. A status can only ever be one of these four fixed
states:
• Pending
• Shipped
• Delivered

• Cancelled
Rules for the status:
• An order normally starts as Pending.
• When it is added to the shipping list, it becomes Shipped.
• When it is marked as delivered, it becomes Delivered.
• If the order is cancelled, it becomes Cancelled.
• No fifth state may ever exist, and it must be impossible for a programmer to
accidentally set a status such as "delivred", "Shipped " or "done". A typo in
a status must be caught by the compiler, not discovered at runtime.
• Free-form text values must not be used to represent the status.
Review
Attributes: int productId, String customerName, String comment
A simple class with a constructor, getters and toString(). All reviews of all
products are kept together in one single flat group, in the order they were added.
There is no rating field and no nested grouping per product.

