6. Operation Requirements
Add Product
Read the product’s ID, name, price, category and stock quantity. The product must
afterwards be visible in the shop’s product listing and be directly reachable from its
ID. Product IDs must be unique — adding a product with an existing ID must be

rejected. The product’s category must also be registered among the shop’s known
categories, without creating a duplicate category.
Remove Product
Remove a product using its ID. If the product does not exist, display an appropriate
message.
Consistency rule: Your program will hold the same products in more than one
place — one arrangement for listing them and another for reaching them instantly
by ID. These two views must never disagree. Whenever a product is deleted, it must
disappear from every place that holds it, in the same operation; otherwise a search
by ID will still return a product that is no longer in the shop. Write a single private
helper method (for example deleteProductEverywhere(int id)) and call it from
every operation that deletes a product.
Display All Products
Display all products in the shop, in the order they were added.
Search Product by ID
Ask for an ID and display the matching product if it exists. This lookup must go
straight to the product; it must not scan through the whole shop.
Show All Categories
Display every distinct category currently known to the shop. Each category appears
exactly once. Any display sequence is acceptable.
Display Products Ordered by Price
Display all products from cheapest to most expensive.
• Use the product’s own default ordering rule (see the Product class). The
sorting code here must not describe how to compare two products — that
knowledge already lives inside Product.
• Sort a copy. The shop’s own product listing must still be in its original order
afterwards.
Create Order
Read an order ID and a customer name. Order IDs must be unique. The new order
starts as Pending and is added to the permanent order record, where it stays for its
whole lifetime.

Add Item to Order
Find the order by its ID, find the requested product by its ID, read the quantity and
add the item to the order.
• The product must exist in the shop; otherwise reject the operation.
• Items must not be added to an order that is already Shipped, Delivered or
Cancelled.
• If the same product is already in the order, do not add a second entry —
increase the quantity of the existing entry instead.
• After the item is added, the order’s total must be recalculated automatically
inside addItem(). The user must never trigger the calculation manually.
Remove Item from Order
Find the order and remove the requested item from it. Items must not be modified
once the order is Shipped, Delivered or Cancelled. After the removal, the total must
be recalculated automatically inside removeItem().
Display Order
Display the customer name, order ID, status, every item with its quantity and
subtotal, and the final total.
Add Order to the Shipping List
Find the order by its ID and place it at the back of the shipping list, then change its
status from Pending to Shipped.
• The same order must never appear twice in the shipping list.
• An order with no items in it cannot be placed in the shipping list.
Ship Next Order
Take the order that has been waiting longest in the shipping list, remove it from
that list, change its status to Delivered and record it among the delivered orders. It
stays in the permanent order record as well.
Before processing, check two things:
1. The shipping list is not empty.
2. The order at the front of the list actually contains at least one item.
An order with no items must not be marked as delivered — display an appropriate
message (for example “This order has no items and cannot be shipped”) and leave it
in the shipping list.
An order must never be shipped or delivered twice.

Cancel Order
Let the store cancel an order that has not been delivered yet.
• Ask the user for an order ID. Find it in the permanent order record; if it does
not exist, display an appropriate message and stop.
• If the order is already Delivered or already Cancelled, reject the cancellation
with an appropriate message.
• If the order is Shipped, it must also be taken out of the shipping list, since a
cancelled order should no longer be waiting to ship.
• If the order is Pending, it was never queued, so there is nothing to take out.
• In every valid case, set the order’s status to Cancelled.
• The order must stay in the permanent order record forever, and it must
never be recorded among the delivered orders — it was cancelled, not
delivered.
Search Order by ID
Find and display any order by its ID, whatever its status is. This lookup must go
straight to the order rather than scanning every order in the system.
Add Review to a Product
Read a product ID, a customer name and a comment, then store the review. A review
may only be added for a product ID that exists in the shop.
Show All Reviews for a Product
Ask for a product ID, then walk through all stored reviews and display only those
written about that product.
Remove Out-of-Stock Products
Delete every product whose stock quantity is 0 from the shop, in a single pass.
• You must remove the products during the same loop that examines them.
Collecting them first and deleting them afterwards is not accepted, and
neither is a plain enhanced for loop — attempting the deletion that way will
crash your program at runtime, and part of this task is to discover why and to
use the mechanism that makes deletion-while-looping legal.
• Reminder: deleting a product from the shop’s listing does not automatically
make it unreachable by ID. After removing it, the same product must also
become impossible to find through Search Product by ID. Reuse the same
helper method described in Remove Product.
Display Orders Ordered by Total
Display all orders sorted by their total value.

• This is a different ordering rule from the one products use, and it must live
outside the Order class, as its own separate ordering rule that can be handed
to the sorting code.
• Product’s own default ordering must not be touched or changed to make this
work.
• Sort a copy — the permanent order record must not be disturbed.

7. Order Lifecycle
An order moves through these states while always remaining in the permanent
order record:
• Create Order → Pending
• Add Order to the Shipping List → Shipped
• Ship Next Order → Delivered
• Cancel Order → Cancelled
The permanent record contains the order in every state. The shipping list holds only
orders still waiting to ship — an order leaves it either by being shipped or by being
cancelled. The delivered-orders record holds delivered orders in delivery sequence.

