9. Important Rules
• Do not use plain arrays to hold the store’s data.
• Product IDs and order IDs must be unique.
• Every order ever created must remain findable for the whole run of the
program, including delivered and cancelled ones.

• An order cannot be shipped when nothing is waiting to be shipped.
• An order cannot be shipped if it contains no items, even if it is next in line.
• An order must never be shipped or delivered twice.
• An item cannot be added to an order if the product does not exist in the shop.
• Adding the same product twice to one order must increase the quantity, not
create a duplicate entry.
• Delivered or cancelled orders must not be modified.
• An order cannot be cancelled twice, and a delivered order cannot be
cancelled.
• The order total must always be recalculated automatically whenever items
are added or removed.
• Out-of-stock products must be deleted during the loop that examines them,
not afterwards.
• An order’s status must be impossible to set to an invalid value; a wrong
status must be a compile-time error.
• Looking up a product or an order by ID must not require scanning through
everything.
• The program must survive invalid IDs and empty data without crashing.
• Keep the program console-based; no database and no graphical interface.
